        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent admission of your 85-year-old female patient, who presented to our department on 26 December 2003 with an acute exacerbation of her known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, she exhibited increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray confirmed hyperinflation without pneumonic infiltrates. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed hypoxaemic respiratory insufficiency. Blood tests showed mild systemic inflammation. She was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and supportive intravenous fluids. Clinical monitoring including pulse oximetry and vital signs was performed throughout her stay. She responded well to treatment with clear symptomatic improvement. Discharge diagnosis is COPD with acute exacerbation (ICD-10: J44.1). I recommend continuation of optimised inhaler therapy and close respiratory follow-up in your practice. Yours sincerely, Dr. [Treating Physician]</div>"
            },
